libFLAC: restoring a corrupt frame wraps rather than overflows, and nothing is exempt from the check for it

The decoder chooses the 32-bit FLAC__fixed_restore_signal and FLAC__lpc_restore_signal when a subframe's bits per sample and coefficients say that the prediction fits in 32 bits, and the 33-bit versions by the same reasoning in 64; nothing holds the residual of a corrupt frame to that premise, so its samples leave the range, and the products and sums of the restore loops overflow, as do the sums and differences of undo_channel_coding. d52921bc exempted all five functions from UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer's signed-integer-overflow check in a fuzzing build unless FUZZING_BUILD_MODE_FLAC_SANITIZE_SIGNED_INTEGER_OVERFLOW is defined, on the ground that the values are audio only; but the overflow is undefined behaviour in every build whatever the values are for, and the exemption hid it from every fuzzer that builds libFLAC as OSS-Fuzz does -- MediaPerch's among them, which build it in that mode for its decoding of frames whose CRC fails and its failing allocations. With the exemption lifted, the corpora of MediaPerch's FLAC fuzzers reach 195 overflowing operations on 103 lines, in all five functions. They are made in unsigned arithmetic of the same width now, which wraps, and the result is the signed number with the same bits -- what two's complement arithmetic gives, and for a frame that decodes correctly exactly what it was: FLAC__fixed_restore_signal and undo_channel_coding cast their operands, as FLAC__fixed_restore_signal_wide casts its own, FLAC__lpc_restore_signal reads its coefficients through an unsigned view of them, so that none of its 109 unrolled products changes, and the 33-bit versions read theirs from an unsigned copy widened to 64 bits; every shift of a sum is made on the signed number, as before. The five exemptions go, so that the check applies in a fuzzing build as in any other. Over 11,900 files -- 218 written by libFLAC's own encoder at 8, 16, 24 and 32 bits, in one, two and six channels, at levels 0, 1, 2, 5 and 8 and with predictors of order 32, of music-like, full-scale and smooth signals, two of them with a 33-bit side channel that only fixed predictors of order 2 and 3 predict; the FLAC fuzzers' corpora and findings; and the codec fuzzer's inputs made into files, which between them reach every line of the five functions but the defaults no order reaches -- the patched library gives the same samples as the original compiled with -fwrapv, in the fuzzing mode and out of it, and under UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer reports nothing where the original reported those 195. It decodes in the time it took: two minutes of 24-bit 96 kHz stereo at level 8 in 183 to 189 ms before and after, and of 16-bit 44.1 kHz at level 5 in 94 to 96, on an i7-1360P at -O3. Against 1.5.0.

